  about it I think both of these forms of  fatalism had to do with the fact that in  John Kennedy's early life he was under  the impression that he would die at an  early age from Addison's disease this is  something that he suffered from until  about 1950 it was almost always fatal  and that was a time when he formed  essentially this personal policy of  living for the moment because tomorrow  you die you talk about two news men in  here that were deeply involved in  American is Minh in liaison one of them  John Scally  with ABC News and was our UN Ambassador  the story's fairly well known although  you suggest that you found out that  maybe that story wasn't really well he  didn't play as bigger roles we think he  played yes I think John Sculley is  absolutely right in what he said all  these years which is that during the  Cuban Missile Crisis he met with a  Soviet intelligence official named  Alexander foe mean those meetings I am  certain took place at the Occidental  restaurant at the Occidental restaurant  the predecessor of the Occidental  restaurant that is now here in  Washington but at the same time what  scollay could not know was who  authorized for me to have these meetings  of anyone and what importance that had  in Moscow what we now know from Soviet  sources as I write in the book is that  far from being authorized by Khrushchev  which is the impression that  skali had a full mean and also the  Americans who knew about these meetings  foe main was freelancing people in the  Soviet embassy during the Cuban Missile  Crisis in Washington had been told  basically to try it to get as much  information as you possibly can  and four main was an example of this and  it turns out that the information he  sent back to Moscow arrived at times  that would not have influenced important  decisions so it turns out to be an  incident interesting vignette but I  think not one that really changed the  course of history  Frank Holman New York Daily News who was  he and why do you key why'd you write so  much about it Frank Holman is still  living as far as I know he was a White  House correspondent for the New York  Daily News and  early 60s he was approached by the  gentleman I referred to earlier gay orgy  [ __ ] off this soviet intelligence  agent in washington whom he knew he was  on the board of the National Press Club  knew [ __ ] off through the Press Club  bolshakov came to Holman and said I want  to meet with Robert Kennedy I know that  you know him and you've covered him  perhaps you could introduce me and it  was Holman who made the introduction  that led to the first meeting between  Robert Kennedy and bolshakov in May of  1961 were you surprised at how active a  role that a news person would have  played in the process yes and to use  news people as intermediaries Holman and  Scalli and others I think shows really  how different the crisis years were from  our own time nowadays President Bush can  pick up a telephone and call mr.  Gorbachev in the early 60s Kennedy and  Khrushchev were reduced to sending  secret messages through news men through  intelligence agents in fact bolshakov  would sometimes meet members of the  kennedy administration on street corners  or in saloons he would pass them a  newspaper inside the newspaper was a  secret private message from Khrushchev  to Kennedy this was a very dicey way to  conduct international relations now  their odds and ends  continuing somewhere I read and had  quote marks around it that Adlai  Stevenson couldn't control the smile on  his face at the death the notice of the  death of John Kennedy that's poignant  the night of the death of John Kennedy  Arthur Schlesinger says he saw Stevenson  at the White House and he said Stevenson  had a smile on his face Schlesinger who  had worked for Stevenson in his  campaigns in the 1950s said I never felt  the same way about Stevenson after that  the relationship between John Kennedy  and Adlai Stevenson is something I go  into a great deal in this book Stevenson  of course had been the nominee of the  Democratic Party in the 50s was still in  1961 when John Kennedy became president  probably the most popular Democrat in  the country more popular among Democrats  than Kennedy was Kennedy was very eager  to cut Stevenson down  sighs he didn't want him as a rival he  also knew that Stephens who could be  very dangerous if Stevenson whom he had  appointed ambassador to the United  Nations should ever decide to resign in  protest over a Kennedy policy I think  another part of this was that Kennedy  felt very emotionally about Stevenson  Kennedy was not someone who was without  a streak of cruelty and he did a number  of things to make Stevenson feel small  and essentially as I I say cut him down  to size  what did you think of Governor Stevenson  as an Illinois and I would be a great  Stephenson fan and Stevenson's name is  almost wholly in Illinois I would say  that his years in the Kennedy  administration were very poignant very  sad a time in which someone who is used  to being the center of attention with a  great deal of influence was a member of  administration that did not like him did  not consult him played cruel tricks on  him who are some of the people that you  got to know in your research for this  book that were around the Kennedy  administration that surprised you as to  how interesting they were some people we  haven't talked about one example was  McGeorge Bundy who was Kennedy's  national security adviser and is now a  writer and scholar on the history of  nuclear weapons he is someone who has a  wonderful sense of humor very smart a  very useful instrument of Kennedy's  diplomacy another one that I've  interviewed is Richard Helms who as I've  suggested earlier now that the Cold War  is over will speak very freely about  many subjects that would have been very  sensitive during this period not least  his involvement in the assassination  attempts against Castro and that raises  sort of a very sensitive and interesting  problem Brian and that is that for all  these years there's been an effort by  people to establish whether or not John  Kennedy knew of and approved of the  assassination attempts by the CIA  against Castro Helms has always been in  a very difficult position because he  prides himself on on being the man who  kept the secrets as a biography of him  was once entitled someone who has not  betrayed the faith that he feel  that he had as a CIA official at the  same time many people around John  Kennedy have made the argument Kennedy  would never have approved of these  militant of these murder attempts  against Castro the CIA was going off on  its own on a rogue operation Helms will  not say that Kennedy approved them yet  at the same time I would say that he is  at least to some extent justifiably  irritated with the fact that the Kennedy  people blame him for something that he  feels John Kennedy authorized one thing  I found in my interviews George Smathers  told me that a month before the Bay of  Pigs invasion in the spring of 61  Kennedy told him there is an effort to  murder Castro that I know about this is  to take place just before a thousand  Cuban exile strained by the CIA hit the  beaches of Cuba trying to overthrow a  Cuban government  if Castro is killed just before those  men hit the beaches there will be chaos  and pandemonium they will have a good  chance of succeeding that to some extent  answers the question of why Kennedy  authorized this very small ragtag group  of invaders that otherwise would seem to  have been impossible to succeed in  invading Cuba how close did we come to  in clear war we came very close  throughout the whole period especially  in 1961 over Berlin Nikita Khrushchev  threatened a number of times to take  over Berlin we the United States and the  West had promised that we would go to  the point of nuclear war to resist that  we were very close then second time was  during the Cuban Missile Crisis October  of 62 the Joint Chiefs of Staff had  resolved if an American plane were  downed in Cuba during that crisis we  would automatically bomb the Soviet  missile sites it took John Kennedy to  stand up against the Joint Chiefs and  say no had he not stood up those sites  would have been bombed Russians would  have been killed  Nikita Khrushchev would have been under  enormous pressure to retaliate against  the United States  another interesting scene was  when mr. Khrushchev I know I'm jumping  all over the place here but there's so  much it's a big book when mr. Khrushchev  got bounced from being the chairman of  the party in the Soviet Union can you  build that little scenario around and  how did he you touched on what his son  had told him that there was a threat  against him but what actually happened  and how did he find out that his days  were over well I think it starts really  with a whole history of his last year  which began with John Kennedy's  assassination when Kennedy was killed in  Dallas the CIA tried to locate  Khrushchev they couldn't find him there  was some worry that Khrushchev was in a  bomb shelter somewhere outside Moscow  bracing for American retaliation for  Soviet involvement in Kennedy's murder  and Khrushchev in fact rushed the next  day the day after the assassination to  the US ambassador in Moscow and said you  must believe me I was not behind this  Kennedy's death removed the argument  that Khrushchev had made with his own  people that you must keep me in power  I'm indispensable because I have this  wonderful relationship with the American  president once John Kennedy died he  could no longer say that and there was a  coup d'etat in 1964 led by Leonid  Brezhnev and this we only now know from  Soviet sources not only did Brezhnev  wanna take Khrushchev out of power and  replace him he also wanted to kill him  he went to the director of the KGB  during that period and said Khrushchev  is scheduled to fly from Cairo to Moscow  why don't you put a bomb aboard the  plane then Brezhnev thought better of  the idea not because he felt any more  sentimental about Khrushchev but because  Andrei Gromyko the Foreign Minister was  on that plane  Brezhnev figured that he would need  Gromyko when he came to power the result  was that in October of 1964 Brezhnev and  other people who Khrushchev thought to  be his closest allies joined together  Khrushchev flew from the Black Sea to  Moscow appeared at a meeting in the  Kremlin they told him he was  he begged to be appointed at least  Minister of Agriculture he did not want  to give up power  they told him essentially forget it and  that was the end of Khrushchev he lived  until 1971 in a small house he was given  outside Moscow he was massively  depressed this was a man who was very  used to power to isolate him for seven  years was almost the worst thing you  could do the picture that you paint  though is mr. Khrushchev being at his  dacha or wherever he was and getting a  phone call from the Politburo that's  right and saying you come to you come to  Moscow he was told to come to Moscow and  Khrushchev and his normally high-handed  way said you know can't you deal with  this when I get back to Moscow next  month nothing should be so important  that I have to interrupt my vacation  they told him that he'd better come now  he was flown to Moscow he got off the  plane rather than the usual welcoming  delegation the only one who was there  was the chairman of the KGB and he had  Chris job driven to this meeting in  Moscow question today with mr. Gorbachev  could that same thing happen  no this coup d'etat depended on the fact  that leaders of the Soviet Union in the  early 1960s were selected by a very  small group if that small group turned  against you you were out a small group  of the same kind tried to throw  Khrushchev out once before in 1957 and  almost succeeded these curves were not  very unusual in those days nowadays as  the Soviet Union becomes more  pluralistic and more people have a say  in who becomes not only the leader of  the Soviet Union but leaders of the  Republic's it's going to be a lot harder  for a group of six men to come together  and simply say you are no longer  employed in the Kremlin is there any new  information to still find out about  Nikita Khrushchev there is it is said  that Khrushchev papers were destroyed  just after Khrushchev was taken out of  power by the order of Leonid Brezhnev  Brezhnev knew that Khrushchev was still  very popular among the Soviet people he  wanted to wipe out any trace of  Khrushchev and therefore not only had  apparently his papers destroyed but also  had the Soviet press and Soviet  publishing and other officials refused  ever to mention the name in Christian  the name Khrushchev in public and  therefore between 1964 and recent years  you've had a whole generation of Soviet  citizens growing up who have never heard  Chris John's name mentioned his Sergei  Khrushchev the son book credible yes it  was written about two years ago and it  was vetted translated and edited by an  eminent scholar American scholar of  Soviet affairs our that Khrushchev  memoirs credible the Khrushchev memoir  is there was some talk when they were  published this was Khrushchev talking  into a tape recorder the tapes were  smuggled to the west and published in  1970 1974 there was some talk at the  time there was there was rarely any  doubt that this was actually Chris Jost  voice there was some talk of the time  that as the price of getting the memoirs  out he was forced to put in some  disinformation that with getting the  memoirs out he was forced to put in some  disinformation that would throw off  Western intelligence my take on them now  is that he told the truth as he knew it  but Chris Geoff's view in many cases was  very distorted he also didn't have  access to his own records your next book  my next book is on Bush and Gorbachev  which I am writing in collaboration with  a journalist strobe Talbott of Time  magazine we are aspiring to write an  account of how this relationship has  developed between these two leaders  between 1989 and 1992 based on  interviews with Soviets and Americans  were expecting it to come out in the  fall of 1992 analyzed question are you  happy with the way this books played out  and the way it's being bought and read I  have this is a book that in many ways  very much disagrees with much of what  has been written about the history of  the crisis years over the last thirty  years many of the people who have  Judit have been people whose work I have  to some extent revised based on both new  information and also additional  perspective and they've treated it  respectfully and for that I'm grateful  Michael Beschloss author of the crisis  years kennedy and khrushchev 1962-1963  thank you for joining us thank you Brian
 

